      "table_no": null,
      "title": "[Governmental groups in China and their territorial control, early 1928]",
      "caption_extra": "Section: Nanking Government. There are at present in China a number of independent and partially independent Governmental groups, whose territorial control may be outlined as follows :—",
      "continued": false,
      "parts": [
        {
          "label": null,
          "columns": [
            "No.",
            "Group",
            "Territory"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "1.",
              "The Ankuochun",
              "Manchuria, Chihli, Shantung, Charhar and uncertain areas on the Mongolian borderland."
            ],
            [
              "2.",
              "The Nanking Government",
              "Kiangsu, Chêkiang, Anhwei, Kiangsi, Fukien."
            ],
            [
              "3.",
              "The Kuominchun",
              "Honan, Shensi, Kansu (in alliance with Nanking)."
            ],
            [
              "4.",
              "Yen Hsi-shan",
              "Shansi (in alliance with the Kuominchun but now uncertain)."
            ],
            [
              "5.",
              "The Kwangsi Military group",
              "Kwangtung, Hunan, Hupeh (opposed to General Chiang Kai-shek personally but not completely broken with Nanking)."
            ],
            [
              "6.",
              "Miscellaneous independent provinces",
              "such as Yunnan, Kweichow, Szechwan."
            ]
          ]
        }
      ],
      "footnotes": [
        "† North China Herald, February 1928, p. 283."
      ],
      "transcriber_notes": [
        "Quoted from the author's article in the North China Daily News; item 6 printed as a single phrase \"Miscellaneous independent provinces such as Yunnan, Kweichow, Szechwan.\", split by transcriber"
